=== lesson
id: py-if
title: if, elif and else
summary: Programs make decisions with `if`: a block of code runs only when a condition is `True`. Add `else` for the other case, and `elif` to check several possibilities in order, where the first true one wins.

=== learn
### Making a decision
Until now, every line of a program ran, top to bottom. An `if` statement lets a program **choose**: some lines run only when a condition is `True`.
```python
temperature = 30
if temperature > 25:
    print("It's hot today")
print("Have a nice day")
```
```output
It's hot today
Have a nice day
```
`temperature > 25` is `True`, so the indented line runs. Change `temperature` to `18` and the condition is `False`: Python skips the indented line, and only `Have a nice day` is printed.

### The parts of an if statement
```python
if temperature > 25:
    print("It's hot today")
```
- `if` is the keyword.
- `temperature > 25` is the **condition**: anything that gives `True` or `False`, like the comparisons from the last lesson.
- The line ends with a **colon** `:`.
- The lines under it, pushed in by **4 spaces**, are the **block** (or **body**). They run only when the condition is `True`.

The block ends at the first line that is back at the left edge. That line, and everything after it, runs either way.

### Indentation decides what's inside
In Python, the spaces at the start of a line aren't decoration: they decide which lines belong to the `if`. Compare:

| Program | When `x` is 3 it prints |
| --- | --- |
| `if x > 5:` / `····print("big")` / `····print("done")` | nothing |
| `if x > 5:` / `····print("big")` / `print("done")` | `done` |

(Each `····` stands for 4 spaces.) In the first program `print("done")` is indented, so it's part of the block and is skipped too. In the second it's back at the left edge, so it always runs.

A block needs at least one line. Forgetting to indent gives an IndentationError:
```output
  File "main.py", line 3
    print("big")
    ^^^^^
IndentationError: expected an indented block after 'if' statement on line 2
```
Use the same 4 spaces for every line in a block. (In the editor on this site, Tab types 4 spaces and Enter after a colon indents the next line for you.)

### else: the other case
`else` gives a block to run when the condition is `False`:
```python
age = 15
if age >= 18:
    print("You can vote")
else:
    print("Too young to vote")
```
```output
Too young to vote
```
Exactly **one** of the two blocks runs, never both and never neither. `else` has no condition of its own (it means "otherwise"), it ends with a colon, and it lines up with its `if`.

### elif: more than two choices
`elif` (short for "else if") adds more conditions to check, in order:
```python
score = 72
if score >= 90:
    print("A")
elif score >= 80:
    print("B")
elif score >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("F")
```
```output
C
```
Python checks the conditions **from the top down** and runs the block of the **first** one that is `True`. Then it skips everything else in the chain, without even checking the remaining conditions:

| Condition | With `score = 72` | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `score >= 90` | `False` | skip, check the next one |
| `score >= 80` | `False` | skip, check the next one |
| `score >= 70` | `True` | print `C`, then leave the chain |
| `else` | not checked | skipped |

`else` at the end catches everything that matched nothing above it. It's optional: without it, a chain where nothing is `True` simply runs no block.

### The order of elif matters
Because the first true condition wins, the order of the checks can change the answer:
```python
score = 95
if score >= 70:
    print("pass")
elif score >= 90:
    print("excellent")
```
```output
pass
```
`95 >= 70` is `True`, so the first block runs and the chain is over. The `excellent` branch can **never** run: every score that is at least 90 is also at least 70, so it's always caught first. Put the most specific (strictest) check first:
```python
score = 95
if score >= 90:
    print("excellent")
elif score >= 70:
    print("pass")
```
```output
excellent
```

### Separate ifs versus one chain
Two `if` statements one after another are **two separate decisions**, and both can run. One `if`/`elif` chain runs **at most one** block:
```python
n = 12
if n > 10:
    print("more than 10")
if n % 2 == 0:
    print("even")
```
```output
more than 10
even
```
If the second `if` were an `elif`, only `more than 10` would print, because the chain stops at its first true condition. Use a chain when the cases are alternatives ("which grade?"), and separate `if`s when they are independent questions ("is it big?" and "is it even?").

### Combining conditions
Any condition from the booleans lesson works after `if`, including `and`, `or`, `not` and chained comparisons:
```python
age = 15
has_ticket = True
if 13 <= age <= 19 and has_ticket:
    print("Teen ticket accepted")
if not has_ticket or age < 13:
    print("Ask an adult")
```
```output
Teen ticket accepted
```

### An if inside an if
A block can contain another `if`. The inner block is indented one more level (8 spaces in total):
```python
age = 20
member = False
if age >= 18:
    if member:
        print("Free entry")
    else:
        print("Ticket: $10")
else:
    print("Under 18s need an adult")
```
```output
Ticket: $10
```
The inner `if` is only checked when the outer condition is `True`. Nesting is useful, but two levels are usually enough; deeper code can often be flattened with `and`.

### Common mistakes
| Mistake | What Python says |
| --- | --- |
| no colon: `if x > 5` | `SyntaxError: expected ':'` |
| one `=`: `if x = 5:` | `SyntaxError: invalid syntax. Maybe you meant '==' or ':=' instead of '='?` |
| a condition on else: `else x < 5:` | `SyntaxError: expected ':'` |
| body not indented | `IndentationError: expected an indented block after 'if' statement on line ...` |
| `elif` without an `if` before it | `SyntaxError: invalid syntax` |

> Values work as conditions too: `if name:` is `True` for any non-empty string and `False` for the empty string `""`. Likewise `0` counts as `False` and every other number as `True`. This is handy, but `if name != "":` says the same thing more clearly while you're learning.

=== keypoints
- `if condition:` runs the indented block (4 spaces) only when the condition is `True`. Lines back at the left edge always run.
- `else:` runs when the condition is `False`: exactly one of the two blocks runs. `else` has no condition.
- `elif` chains are checked **top to bottom**, and only the **first** true branch runs. Put the strictest check first.
- Separate `if` statements are independent and can all run; one `if`/`elif` chain runs at most one block.
- Conditions can use `and`, `or`, `not` and chains like `13 <= age <= 19`. An `if` can sit inside another `if`'s block.
- Missing colons, `=` instead of `==`, and missing indentation are the most common errors.

=== example: Ticket prices
intro: An if/elif/else chain picks exactly one price. Change age and run it again.
```python
age = 15
if age < 5:
    price = 0
elif age < 13:
    price = 4
elif age < 65:
    price = 9
else:
    price = 5
print("Age", age, "pays", price)
```
2: Is 15 less than 5? No, so Python skips this block and moves to the next check.
4: Is 15 less than 13? No, skip again.
6: Is 15 less than 65? Yes, so this is the first true condition.
7: price is set to 9, and the rest of the chain (including else) is skipped.
8-9: else would catch ages 65 and over, but it isn't reached this time.
10: Back at the left edge, so this line always runs: Age 15 pays 9

=== example: Checking a password
intro: An if/else with two separate ifs inside the else block, so every problem gets reported.
```python
password = "sun7"
long_enough = len(password) >= 8
ends_with_digit = password[-1].isdigit()
if long_enough and ends_with_digit:
    print("Password accepted")
else:
    print("Password rejected")
    if not long_enough:
        print("- use at least 8 characters")
    if not ends_with_digit:
        print("- end with a digit")
print("Checked", len(password), "characters")
```
2: The password has 4 characters, so long_enough is False.
3: The last character is 7, a digit, so ends_with_digit is True.
4: and needs both: False and True is False, so the accepted block is skipped.
6-7: else runs instead and prints Password rejected.
8-9: An if inside the else block: not long_enough is True, so this message prints.
10-11: A separate if: not ends_with_digit is False, so this message is skipped.
12: Back at the left edge: this always runs.

=== animation: Which branch runs?
view: vars
intro: Watch the highlighted line jump. Python checks each condition in order and runs only the first true branch.
```python
score = 78
if score >= 90:
    grade = "A"
elif score >= 80:
    grade = "B"
elif score >= 70:
    grade = "C"
else:
    grade = "F"
print("Grade:", grade)
```
1: score will hold 78.
2: First check: is {score} >= 90? That's {score >= 90}, so Python skips this branch's block.
4: Next check: is {score} >= 80? {score >= 80} again, so skip.
6: Is {score} >= 70? {score >= 70}! This is the first true condition.
7: Run this branch's block: grade becomes "C". The else is never checked.
10: The chain is finished, and this line is outside it. It prints Grade: {grade}

=== animation: When order goes wrong
view: vars
intro: The same kind of chain with the checks in the wrong order. Watch which branch catches a score of 95.
```python
score = 95
if score >= 50:
    result = "pass"
elif score >= 90:
    result = "excellent"
else:
    result = "fail"
print(result)
```
1: score will hold 95, which should be excellent.
2: The first check is {score} >= 50, which is {score >= 50}. The first true condition wins...
3: ...so result becomes "pass", and the rest of the chain is skipped.
8: The excellent branch was never even checked. Every score that's at least 90 is also at least 50, so this chain can never give excellent. The strictest check has to come first.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Spot the correct if
prompt: Which of these lines is a correct start of an if statement?
- `if x > 5` :: The colon at the end is missing. Python gives `SyntaxError: expected ':'`.
* `if x > 5:` :: Right: the keyword, a condition, and a colon at the end.
- `if x = 5:` :: One `=` stores a value; it can't be a condition. Comparing needs `==`.
- `If x > 5:` :: Python is case-sensitive, so the keyword must be `if` in small letters.
hint: Check three things: the keyword's spelling, the condition, and the end of the line.
hint: Every line that opens a block ends with the same character.
right: An `if` line is the keyword `if` (small letters), a condition that gives `True` or `False`, and a colon. The colon tells Python that an indented block comes next.
wrong: A correct `if` line has `if` in small letters, a real condition (comparing uses `==`, not `=`), and a colon at the end.

=== task: predict easy
title: Hot or not?
code:
```python
temp = 30
if temp > 25:
    print("hot")
print("done")
```
hint: Is the condition `True` or `False` when `temp` is 30?
hint: Which line is outside the if block?
right: `30 > 25` is `True`, so the indented `print("hot")` runs. `print("done")` is back at the left edge, so it runs either way.
wrong: The condition `30 > 25` is `True`, so the block runs and prints `hot`. Then `print("done")`, which isn't indented, runs no matter what.
wrong[hot]: `print("done")` is not indented, so it's not inside the if. It always runs, after the if is finished.
wrong[done]: `30 > 25` is `True`, so the indented line runs too, before `done`.

=== task: predict easy
title: Even or odd?
code:
```python
n = 7
if n % 2 == 0:
    print("even")
else:
    print("odd")
print("checked", n)
```
hint: What is `7 % 2`?
hint: When the condition is `False`, the else block runs instead.
right: `7 % 2` is `1` (the remainder), so `n % 2 == 0` is `False`. The else block runs and prints `odd`, then the last line always runs.
wrong: `7 % 2` gives the remainder 1, which isn't 0, so the condition is `False` and the else block runs: `odd`. The last line is outside both blocks, so it runs too.
wrong[even\nchecked 7]: `7 % 2` is 1, not 0, so the condition is `False`. That sends Python to the else block.
wrong[odd]: The last line isn't indented, so it isn't part of the else. It always runs and prints `checked 7`.
wrong[even\nodd\nchecked 7]: An if/else runs exactly one of its two blocks, never both.

=== task: predict medium
title: A chain and a separate if
code:
```python
x = 15
if x > 10:
    print("big")
elif x > 5:
    print("medium")
if x > 12:
    print("over 12")
else:
    print("12 or less")
```
hint: Lines 2 to 5 are one chain. Line 6 starts a new, separate if statement.
hint: In a chain only the first true branch runs, even if a later condition is also true.
right: The first chain stops at its first true condition, `x > 10`, so only `big` prints from it (`medium` is skipped even though `15 > 5` is also true). The second if is a separate decision: `15 > 12` is `True`, so it prints `over 12`.
wrong: There are two separate decisions. The first chain prints only `big`, because a chain runs only its first true branch. The second `if` starts fresh: `15 > 12` is `True`, so it prints `over 12`.
wrong[big\nmedium\nover 12]: `elif` belongs to the first chain, and a chain runs only its first true branch. `x > 10` was true, so the `medium` branch is skipped.
wrong[big]: Line 6 starts a new `if`, which isn't part of the first chain. It's checked on its own: `15 > 12` is `True`, so `over 12` prints.
wrong[big\n12 or less]: `x` is 15, and `15 > 12` is `True`, so the second if prints `over 12`.

=== task: parsons medium
title: Positive, negative or zero
prompt: Arrange lines into a program that prints whether `x` is positive, negative or zero. With `x = -3` it should print:
```output
negative
```
Use one if/elif/else chain, and indent the bodies (use the indent buttons). Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
x = -3
if x > 0:
    print("positive")
elif x < 0:
    print("negative")
else:
    print("zero")
```
distractors:
```python
elif x = 0:
else x < 0:
```
hint: The chain starts with `if`, then `elif`, then `else`, each followed by its own indented print.
hint: `else` never has a condition, and comparing uses `==`, not `=`.
right: The chain checks `x > 0` (false), then `x < 0` (true, so it prints `negative`). `else` needs no condition, because it catches the only case left: zero.
wrong: Order the chain as if, elif, else, with each print indented under its own line. The distractors are broken: `else` can't have a condition, and `x = 0` stores a value instead of comparing.

=== task: fill medium
title: Build the chain
prompt: Fill the three blanks with the keywords `if`, `elif` and `else` so that this is **one** chain, and only one grade is printed. With `score = 95` it should print:
```output
A
```
code:
```python
score = 95
?? score >= 90:
    print("A")
?? score >= 80:
    print("B")
??:
    print("C")
```
answer: if | elif | else
hint: A chain starts with one keyword, continues with another, and ends with a third.
hint: The last line has no condition, so it can only be one keyword.
right: `if` starts the chain, `elif` adds a second condition to the **same** chain, and `else` (no condition) catches everything else. With 95, the first branch prints `A` and the rest of the chain is skipped.
wrong: A chain is `if` → `elif` → `else`. Only `if` can start it, `elif` continues it, and `else` ends it without a condition.
wrong[if | if | else]: Two `if`s make two separate decisions. 95 is both `>= 90` and `>= 80`, so this prints `A` and then `B`. Using `elif` joins the second check to the chain, so it's skipped once `A` has been printed.
wrong[elif | elif | else]: A chain can't start with `elif`: there's no `if` for it to continue, so Python reports `SyntaxError: invalid syntax`.
wrong[if | else | elif]: `else` must come last and has no condition. Here it has a condition, `score >= 80`, which is a SyntaxError.

=== task: cells medium
title: Grade four scores
prompt: For each score, which letter does this code print?
code:
```python
if score >= 90:
    print("A")
elif score >= 80:
    print("B")
elif score >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("F")
```
labels: score = 95 | score = 80 | score = 79 | score = 45
answer: A | B | C | F
wrong[A | C | C | F]: 80 is caught by `score >= 80`, because `>=` includes 80 itself: `80 >= 80` is `True`. So it's a B.
wrong[A | B | B | F]: 79 isn't `>= 80`, so the B branch is skipped. The next check, `79 >= 70`, is true: C.
wrong[A | B | C | C]: 45 fails every condition, so none of the if/elif blocks run, and the else catches it: F.
hint: For each score, go down the chain and stop at the first condition that's `True`.
hint: `>=` includes the equal case: `80 >= 80` is `True`.
right: 95 stops at the first check (A); 80 fails `>= 90` but passes `>= 80` (B); 79 first passes at `>= 70` (C); 45 fails every condition, so else catches it (F).
wrong: Test each score against the conditions in order and stop at the first true one. Remember `>=` includes the boundary, so 80 is a B and 79 is a C.

=== task: code medium
title: Price a ticket
prompt: Set `price` according to `age`:
- under 5: `0`
- 5 to 12: `4`
- 13 to 64: `9`
- 65 and over: `5`

`age` is already set before your code runs, and the checks try several different ages, so don't set `age` yourself. Use one if/elif/else chain.
starter:
```python
# age is already set: write the chain that sets price
price = 0
```
solution:
```python
if age < 5:
    price = 0
elif age < 13:
    price = 4
elif age < 65:
    price = 9
else:
    price = 5
```
tests:
- price
cases:
- age = 3
- age = 5
- age = 12
- age = 13
- age = 40
- age = 64
- age = 65
- age = 90
fail[2]: Age 5 should cost 4. "Under 5" means `age < 5`, so 5 itself is not free.
fail[3]: Age 12 should cost 4: the second band is 5 to 12. Check it with `age < 13` (or `age <= 12`).
fail[4]: Age 13 should cost 9. If 13 got 4, your second check includes 13: use `age < 13`.
fail[6]: Age 64 should cost 9; only 65 and over pay 5. Use `age < 65` for the third band.
fail[7]: Age 65 should cost 5. Check that your third band stops before 65 (`age < 65`), so 65 falls through to the else.
error[NameError]: Don't create or rename `age`: it's already set before your code runs. Make sure you create `price` in every branch.
hint: Check the bands from youngest to oldest: `if age < 5:` first.
hint: Because the chain stops at the first true condition, `elif age < 13:` only sees ages that are 5 or more.
hint: After `elif age < 65:` the only ages left are 65 and over, so `else:` handles them.
right: Each condition only has to check the upper limit, because the earlier branches already caught the younger ages: by the time `age < 13` is checked, `age` is known to be at least 5. That's what makes an elif chain so tidy.
wrong: Check the bands in order with one chain: `if age < 5`, `elif age < 13`, `elif age < 65`, `else`. Each branch sets `price`. The boundaries matter: 5, 13 and 65 belong to the higher band.

=== task: predict hard
title: Nested decisions
code:
```python
age = 20
member = False
coupon = True
if age >= 18:
    if member or coupon:
        print("discount")
    else:
        print("full price")
    if not member:
        print("join today")
else:
    print("kids price")
print("thanks")
```
hint: Start with the outer if: is `age >= 18`? Then work through everything indented under it.
hint: Inside the outer block there are two separate decisions: an if/else, then a second if.
hint: The last line isn't indented at all.
right: `20 >= 18` is `True`, so the outer block runs. Inside it, `member or coupon` is `False or True`, which is `True`: `discount`. The second inner `if` is separate, and `not member` is `True`: `join today`. The outer `else` is skipped, and `thanks` always prints.
wrong: Go level by level. The outer condition is `True`, so its block runs and the outer `else` is skipped. Inside, the if/else prints `discount` (`or` needs only one `True`), then the separate inner `if` prints `join today`. Finally `thanks`, which is outside everything.
wrong[discount\nthanks]: The block under the outer `if` contains two decisions. After the if/else, the separate `if not member:` is checked too, and `not False` is `True`, so `join today` prints.
wrong[full price\njoin today\nthanks]: `member or coupon` is `False or True`. `or` needs only one side to be `True`, so the condition is `True` and `discount` prints.
wrong[discount\njoin today]: `print("thanks")` is at the left edge, outside every block, so it always runs.

=== task: code hard
title: Leap years
prompt: A year is a **leap year** if it can be divided by 4 with no remainder, **except** years divisible by 100, which are not leap years, **unless** they are also divisible by 400.

So 2024 is a leap year, 2023 isn't, 1900 isn't (divisible by 100), but 2000 is (divisible by 400).

`year` is already set before your code runs. Set `leap` to `True` or `False`.
starter:
```python
# year is already set: set leap to True or False
leap = False
```
solution:
```python
if year % 400 == 0:
    leap = True
elif year % 100 == 0:
    leap = False
elif year % 4 == 0:
    leap = True
else:
    leap = False
```
tests:
- leap
cases:
- year = 2024
- year = 2023
- year = 1900
- year = 2000
- year = 2100
- year = 1996
- year = 2400
fail[1]: 2024 is divisible by 4 (and not by 100), so it is a leap year.
fail[3]: 1900 is divisible by 100 but not by 400, so it is **not** a leap year. Check the 400 rule and the 100 rule before the 4 rule.
fail[4]: 2000 is divisible by 400, so it **is** a leap year, even though it's also divisible by 100. The 400 check must come first.
fail[5]: 2100 is divisible by 100 but not by 400: not a leap year.
fail[7]: 2400 is divisible by 400: a leap year.
error[NameError]: Don't create or rename `year`: it's already set before your code runs. Make sure every branch sets `leap`.
hint: "Divisible by 4" means `year % 4 == 0`: the remainder is 0.
hint: Check the most specific rule first: divisible by 400 means leap, whatever else is true.
hint: Then divisible by 100 means not leap; then divisible by 4 means leap; anything else is not.
right: Ordering the checks from most specific to most general makes each rule simple: 400 wins over everything, then 100, then 4. In a chain, a later branch only sees the years that no earlier branch caught.
wrong: Test the rules from most specific to least: `if year % 400 == 0` → leap; `elif year % 100 == 0` → not leap; `elif year % 4 == 0` → leap; `else` → not leap. Checking 4 first would wrongly make 1900 a leap year.
